insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 1, '`forecastIntervals` is called with damped on EKENE-P1, h 12 and seed 11, and `nSims` is left out. How many paths are simulated, and how does its point forecast relate to the fitted method''s?', '["100 paths, the fewest the engine takes without a warning, and the point forecast is their P50", "None: `nSims` has no default, so the call is refused with the field `nSims` named", "1000 paths, and the point forecast is exactly the h-step forecast `fitSmoothing` returns", "1000 paths, and the point forecast is the mean of the simulated rates at each step"]'::jsonb, 2, '`nSims` is 1000 when it is left out, and the point forecast is exactly `fitSmoothing`''s, so asking for an interval changes nothing about it. The P50 is a percentile of the paths (137.469798 at step 12 against a point forecast of 169.556510), and 1 path is accepted with no warning. It is the seed that has no default. The engine returns no mean of the paths; the point forecast comes from the fitted final state.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 2, 'The teaching bootstrap''s basis says each residual is drawn "with replacement from the 46 scored in-sample residuals (index floor(u x 46), u from mulberry32(11), path by path, step by step)". In what order does the stream feed the draws?', '["Path one takes a draw for every one of its steps, then path two begins, and so on", "Step 1 is drawn for all 1000 paths first, then step 2 for all of them, and so on to step 12", "Each path runs its own stream, seeded 11 plus the path number, so no order is shared", "All the draws are sorted before use, so the order the stream is read in changes nothing"]'::jsonb, 0, '"Path by path, step by step" means path one takes all h of its draws before path two takes any. Drawing step 1 across every path first is the other order, and it would hand each path different residuals from the same seed. There is one mulberry32 stream on the seed given, shared by every path. Nothing is sorted before use; the percentiles are read from the sorted simulated rates after the paths are drawn.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 3, 'Two bootstrap runs differ only in their seed, 11 against 12, and their middle percentiles at the last step read 137.469798 and 140.453203. How does the course read the pair?', '["Seed 12 is the better run, since its P50 lies nearer the point forecast of 169.556510", "As a fault, because one seeded stream should give the same percentiles whatever the seed", "Seed 11 is the one to trust, since the engine falls back to it when no seed is passed", "As two draws of the same experiment, each quoted with its seed and its paths"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A different seed is a different stream, and the percentiles move with it; neither seed is the better answer, so a bootstrap figure travels with its seed and nSims. Nearness to the point forecast is no test: the residuals can pull the middle of the paths away from it. The same call with the same seed returns the same percentiles bit for bit, which is what one stream promises. The seed has no default, so the engine falls back to nothing.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 4, 'A call to `forecastIntervals` gives the method, the series and h, and leaves the seed out. What comes back?', '["An interval drawn on seed 0, which the engine takes as the seed when none is passed to it", "A refusal naming `seed`, in the engine''s words \"seed must be a whole number from 0 to 4294967295\"", "An interval seeded from the clock, with a warning that the figures cannot be reproduced", "A refusal naming `nSims`: \"nSims must be a whole number from 1 to 100000\", checked with the seed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The seed has no default, and leaving it out is refused with the field `seed` named, in the words quoted. Seed 0 is accepted when it is given, and it is never filled in. Every draw comes from one mulberry32 stream on the seed given, so no clock enters and no warning of that kind exists. `nSims` has its own rule and its own default of 1000, so leaving the seed out never brings its message.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 5, 'Damped on EKENE-P1, h 12, seed 11: the P50 at step 12 is 132.416487 with 100 paths, 137.469798 with 1000 and 135.920627 with 10000. What does the course draw from the three?', '["The P50 ought to rise with every tenfold step in paths, so the 10000 run shows a defect", "More paths steady the percentiles, and they do not make the method right", "At 10000 paths the percentile has settled exactly, so it may be quoted without its seed", "The 1000 run is right because it is the default and it matches the point forecast"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The three figures wander and settle as the paths grow, which is all more paths can do: the method and its residuals are unchanged. No rule says a percentile rises with the path count. Any figure from the bootstrap, at any count, is quoted with its seed and nSims, because a different seed moves it. The point forecast at step 12 is 169.556510, which none of the three matches, and the default carries no special truth.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 6, 'At step 1 one path of the damped bootstrap draws a large negative residual. What does that draw do to the path''s later steps?', '["Nothing, since every step restarts from the fitted final level and trend", "The path''s next draws come only from the positive residuals, to bring it back toward the point forecast", "The path is dropped and redrawn, as a rate more than one residual below the forecast is refused", "Its simulated rate pulls the level down and makes the trend more negative, so later steps start lower and fall faster"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The simulated rate plays the part of y_t in the recursion and updates the state, so the level moves toward it by alpha and the trend moves toward the change in level by beta; the next one-step forecast starts from there. Restarting every step from the fitted state would stop draws accumulating, and the widening intervals show they do. Every draw is uniform over the whole pool, with replacement. No path is dropped or refused for its size.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 7, 'Across the 1000 paths of the teaching bootstrap, which quantities change from one path to the next?', '["The simulated rates and the level and trend states; alpha, beta and phi stay fitted", "alpha, beta and phi, refitted on each simulated path, so the paths carry parameter uncertainty", "Only the residual at step 1; every later step follows the point forecast''s own recursion", "The residual pool, which the seed redraws from the series for every path in turn"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The parameters are fitted once, on the whole series, and held for every path; only the states and the simulated rates move. The course lists parameter uncertainty in the bootstrap among what is not built, so nothing is refitted per path. Each step of each path adds its own draw, and the pool is fixed: the scored in-sample residuals of the one fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 8, 'The teaching bootstrap reports `poolSize` 46 on EKENE-P1''s 48 months. Which two months have no residual in the pool, and why?', '["Months 46 and 47, the last two, held back to check the bootstrap on months it never saw", "The two largest residuals in size, trimmed so that one bad month cannot widen every interval", "Month 0, which has no one-step forecast, and month 1, spent on the start of the damped method", "Month 0 and month 47, the first and the last, whose residuals the engine treats as edge effects"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pool is the scored residuals from `scoredFrom` on. Month 0 has nothing before it to forecast from, and damped starts with l_1 = y_1 and b_1 = y_2 - y_1, so month 1 is forecast exactly and not scored: 48 less 2 is 46. No month is held back from the pool, no residual is trimmed for its size, and the last month is scored like any other.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 9, 'ses is fitted on the same 48 months of EKENE-P1 and bootstrapped. How many residuals are in its pool?', '["46, the same as damped, since the pool size depends on the series length alone", "48, one per month, counting month 0''s residual as 0", "45, since ses also sets aside the last month to fix its flat forecast", "47, as ses spends only month 0 and scores from index 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ses starts at l_1 = y_1 and has no trend to start, so it scores 47 errors on 48 months and its pool is those 47. The pool depends on the method''s start as well as the length, which is why damped has 46. Month 0 has no residual at all; it is null. The flat forecast is the final level, and the last month is scored like the others.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 10, 'EKENE-P6 has 3 months. `fitSmoothing` fits holt on them. What does `forecastIntervals` do with the same holt call?', '["It refuses, naming `h`, because a series of 3 months cannot carry a forecast of more than 3 steps ahead", "It refuses, naming `y`: 3 values leave 1 scored residual, and the bootstrap resamples at least 2", "An interval whose percentiles all equal the point forecast, drawn from the one residual", "Refused with `nSims` named, because 1000 paths need a pool at least as large as h"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s words: "y has 3 values, which leave 1 scored residual: the bootstrap resamples at least 2, so ''holt'' needs at least 4 values here". h is limited by the cap of 10000 alone; the series length does not enter. A pool of one would give every path the same draw, and the engine refuses it rather than return such an interval. The pool size is checked against 2 and has no link to nSims or h.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 11, 'With an `initialTrend` given, how few values does `forecastIntervals` accept for holt, and why is it one fewer than without?', '["3, because month 1 is no longer spent on the start, so its residual is scored", "4, since a given trend changes the start and the bootstrap still counts the second month as spent", "2, since an initialTrend and the first value together fix both states before any residual", "5, because a given trend is checked against two scored residuals of its own"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With an initialTrend the start is l_1 = y_1 and b_1 = initialTrend, so y_2 is forecast and scored; 3 values then leave 2 scored residuals, the least the bootstrap resamples. Counting month 1 as spent is the rule without an initialTrend, which is why that case needs 4. Two values would fit holt with one scored error, a pool of 1, and be refused. No check of the given trend exists.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 12, 'The 46 residuals of the teaching bootstrap have a mean of -1.726983 bbl/d. What does the engine do with that mean before it draws from them?', '["Subtracts it from each residual, so the draws average 0 and the P50 follows the point forecast", "Adds it to the point forecast, so the reported point forecast is shifted down by that amount", "Nothing: the residuals are drawn as fitted, so the paths carry whatever lean they have", "Reports it as a warning and removes the residuals below the mean from the pool"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis says so in the engine''s own words: "residuals are drawn as fitted without centring (their mean is not subtracted)". Subtracting the mean is the centred bootstrap, the alternative in common use that the engine did not take. The point forecast is exactly the fitted method''s and is never shifted. No warning is raised and no residual is removed.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 13, 'ses on EKENE-P1, h 12, seed 11, 1000 paths, nonNegative false: the point forecast is 211.400000 at every step, yet at step 12 the P10 (high) of the paths is 69.250000. What explains it?', '["With nonNegative false the percentiles are read from the negative side of the paths, which swaps the high and the low labels at every step", "The flat point forecast comes from the P50 at step 1, 198.900000, so it drifts away from the paths", "alpha fits to 1, so each residual is a month-to-month change of the decline, mean -20.525532, and every path replays that fall", "Seed 11 gives ses a run of negative draws that another seed would not, so the figure is chance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With alpha at 1 each one-step forecast is the month before, so each residual is one month''s change of the decline, and their mean is -20.525532 bbl/d. Drawn as fitted, they push every path down step after step, while the flat method''s point forecast stays at the last rate. nonNegative only decides whether a negative percentile is reported as 0; it never swaps labels. The point forecast is the final level, and the P50 at step 1 is itself 198.900000. A lean that large is the method''s residuals, whatever the seed.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 14, 'In the teaching run the P50 starts a little above the point forecast (206.724356 against 204.969642) and ends well below it (137.469798 against 169.556510). Where should a reader take the middle of the paths from?', '["From the percentiles, step by step; the point forecast is the path that draws 0 at every step and need not sit in the middle", "The P50 and the point forecast are one figure computed two ways, so the point forecast is the middle of the paths", "As the mean of the P90 (low) and the P10 (high), which the engine returns as each interval''s centre", "Where the step 1 P50 sits, carried forward, since later steps only widen the interval around it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A path that draws 0 at every step follows the fitted recursion exactly, so the point forecast is that path; the paths lean with the residuals, and the P50 shows where their middle sits at each step. The two figures differ at both steps quoted, so they are not one figure. The engine returns no mid-point of the P90 and P10. The P50 moves at every step, from 206.724356 to 137.469798.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-residual-bootstrap', 15, 'Which values of `nSims` does `forecastIntervals` accept?', '["Any whole number of 100 or more, since fewer paths give no stable percentile", "Whole numbers from 1 to 10000, the same cap the engine puts on h and on a horizon", "Even whole numbers only, so that each percentile can be the mean of two simulated values", "Whole numbers from 1 to 100000, with 1000 used when it is left out"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The boundary is 1 and 100000 accepted, 0 and 100001 refused, in the engine''s words "nSims must be a whole number from 1 to 100000", and the default is 1000. A small count is legal; its percentiles simply move more. 10000 is the cap on h and the horizon, a different rule. An odd count is accepted, and the quantile rule then reads a single simulated value.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 1, 'The platform''s quantile is applied to the sorted values 1, 2, ..., 10. What is their 10th percentile, and which case of the rule gives it?', '["1, the smallest value, because an index of 1 names the 1st smallest outright", "1.500000, the mean of the 1st and 2nd smallest, since idx 1 is whole and n is even", "2, the 2nd smallest, because a whole index takes the (idx+1)-th smallest for any n", "Whatever linear interpolation between the 1st and 2nd sorted values gives at a p of 0.1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'idx = n x p = 10 x 0.1 = 1, a whole number, and n is even, so the rule takes the mean of the idx-th and (idx+1)-th smallest: 1.500000. Taking the 1st smallest outright is the case of a fractional index. The (idx+1)-th smallest is the rule for a whole index with n odd. Linear interpolation is the numpy default, a different rule the engine does not use.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 2, 'A bootstrap is run with nSims 9. Which of the 9 simulated values at a step is reported as the P90 (low)?', '["The mean of the 1st and 2nd smallest, the case every path count shares", "The largest of the 9, reading the 90 in the label as the 90th percentile of the paths", "A value interpolated 90 percent of the way from the 1st smallest to the 2nd", "The smallest, since idx 0.9 is not whole and the ceil(idx)-th smallest is taken"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The P90 (low) is the 10th percentile. idx = 9 x 0.1 = 0.9 is fractional, so the rule takes the ceil(idx)-th smallest, the 1st: the course''s table gives 1.000000 for the values 1 to 9. The mean of two values is the case of a whole index with an even count. The largest value would be the high end, which the label does not name. Interpolation is the numpy rule.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 3, 'Why is every percentile of a bootstrap at the default 1000 paths the mean of two simulated values?', '["1000 x 0.1, 1000 x 0.5 and 1000 x 0.9 are whole and 1000 is even, so the idx-th and (idx+1)-th are averaged", "The engine averages each path with the next one before it sorts, to halve the noise of the draws", "Paths are drawn in pairs from the stream, and each pair gives a single simulated value at every step", "The rule always averages two values whatever nSims is, as linear interpolation does"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 1000 paths every index, 100, 500 and 900, is whole and the count is even, so each percentile is the mean of two neighbouring sorted values; at step 1 of the teaching run the P90 (low) 174.139087 is the mean of the 100th and 101st smallest. No path is averaged with another, and paths are drawn one at a time, step by step. With an odd count or a fractional index the rule reads a single value.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 4, 'The teaching run is repeated with 999 paths on the same seed. What changes in the way each percentile is read?', '["Nothing at all, since 999 and 1000 both give whole indices at 0.1, 0.5 and 0.9, so each percentile is still the mean of two values", "The call is refused, because nSims must be even for a mean of two values to exist", "Every index turns fractional, so each percentile is a single simulated value, and the 999 paths are the first 999 of the 1000", "A fresh stream is drawn for an odd count, so the 999 paths share no draw with the 1000 on that seed, and every percentile moves for that alone"]'::jsonb, 2, '999 x 0.1 is 99.900000, and likewise at 0.5 and 0.9 the index is not whole, so each percentile is the ceil(idx)-th smallest value; the table gives 100.000000 at p 0.1 for 999 sorted values. The stream runs path by path, so the first 999 paths are the same draws as before and the comparison isolates the rule and one path; the seed alone starts the stream, whatever the count. An odd nSims is accepted; the range is 1 to 100000.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 5, '`forecastIntervals` returns arrays keyed `P90`, `P50` and `P10`. Which percentile of the simulated paths sits under the key `P90`?', '["The 10th percentile, the low case, which 90 percent of the paths meet or exceed", "The 90th percentile, the high case, which only a tenth of the paths exceed", "The 90th percentile of the residual pool, added to the point forecast at each step", "Either percentile, since nonNegative decides which end of the paths the label names"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Production is an outcome where more is better, and the platform labels outcomes by exceedance, so P90 is the low case, the 10th percentile of the simulated paths. The 90th percentile is P10 (high). The percentiles are read from the simulated rates at each step; the residual pool only feeds the draws. nonNegative only reports a negative percentile as 0; it never moves a label.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 6, 'The platform''s definition reads: "P90 means a 90% probability the actual quantity meets or exceeds this value, per SPE PRMS." Why does that put P90 at the low end of a set of simulated rates?', '["Production is taken as an outcome where less is better, so the platform reverses the percentile", "The engine sorts the simulated rates from the highest to the lowest before it reads the quantile", "Only reserves follow that convention, and the engine extends it by flipping the sign of each rate", "A rate that 90 percent of the paths meet or exceed leaves only a tenth of them below it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Meeting or exceeding a value with probability 0.9 means only a tenth of the outcomes fall below it, which is the 10th percentile: the low case. The engine''s basis says production is an outcome where more is better. The rule reads the idx-th smallest values, sorted from the lowest. No sign is flipped anywhere; the convention is the platform''s for every outcome it reports.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 7, 'A report prints, for one step of a bootstrap, P90 (low) 225.708211 beside P10 (high) 174.139087. What does the course conclude?', '["The paths drifted upward at that step, and such a drift can lift the low case above the high case", "The labels were swapped somewhere between the engine and the page, since P90 never exceeds P10", "nonNegative clipped the P10 (high) at that step, so the two figures are legal as they stand", "With few distinct residuals the quantile rule may order the two percentiles either way round"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 10th percentile of a set of values can never exceed its 90th, so P90 (low) is at or below P50, and P50 at or below P10 (high), at every step; the course checks it on the teaching run, where step 1 reads 174.139087 low and 225.708211 high. A drift moves every percentile together without reordering them. Clipping only raises a negative percentile to 0. The quantile rule reads sorted values, so its order is fixed.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 8, 'The teaching bootstrap, damped on EKENE-P1 with seed 11 and 1000 paths, reports `clippedToZero` 3. What are the three?', '["Three paths that fell below zero and were removed before any percentile was read", "Three steps at which the damped point forecast went below zero and was reported as 0", "The P90 (low) at steps 10, 11 and 12, each negative as simulated and reported as 0", "The P90, the P50 and the P10 at step 12, all three below zero and reported as 0 together"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With nonNegative true, a percentile below 0 is reported as 0 and counted; on the teaching run that happens to the P90 (low) at steps 10, 11 and 12, and no P50 or P10 is touched. No path is removed: the rule acts on percentiles after they are read. The point forecast is never clipped, and damped''s is 169.556510 at step 12. The P50 at step 12 is 137.469798.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 9, 'Holt on EKENE-P5, h 12, seed 11, 1000 paths reports 11 of its 36 percentiles as 0. At step 12 the P90 reads 0.000000 with nonNegative true and -58.408958 with it false. What is the P50 at step 12 in the two runs?', '["0.000000 with nonNegative true, since the clipping resets the whole interval at that step", "138.941534 in both, since only a negative percentile is changed", "138.941534 with it false, and 2.887394, the point forecast, with it true", "Lower with nonNegative true, as paths clipped to 0 pull the median of the paths down"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule changes only a percentile below 0, and the P50 at step 12 is 138.941534 either way; the paths are simulated in full both times. The interval is not reset, and the P50 is never replaced by the point forecast, which is 2.887394. Paths are never clipped, only reported percentiles, so nothing pulls the median.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 10, 'A bootstrap reports the P90 (low) at some step as 0. What does that reported 0 say?', '["At least a tenth of the paths fell below zero at that step: the method''s paths ran out of rate", "The well is forecast to be shut in from that step on, at a rate of exactly zero", "A zero rate in the series itself was carried forward into the interval at that step", "Every one of the paths reached zero at that step, so the interval has collapsed onto one single value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The P90 (low) is the 10th percentile of the paths, so a negative one means at least a tenth of the paths ended below zero, and nonNegative reports it as 0 and counts it. It is no forecast of a shut-in and no rate the engine measured. The other percentiles at that step are read from the same paths and are usually far above 0; on the teaching run the P10 (high) at step 12 is 324.803671.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 11, 'Holt fitted on EKENE-P5 crosses zero between step 16 and step 17. With nonNegative true and h 24, what point forecast does `forecastIntervals` return at step 17?', '["0.000000, reported as 0 and counted in `clippedToZero`", "0.078646, the last value above zero, held from step 16 on", "None: a point forecast below zero ends the call with a refusal naming `h`", "-0.623541, as the point forecast is never clipped"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The point forecast of `forecastIntervals` is exactly the fitted method''s h-step forecast, and the zero rule acts on percentiles only; holt''s line is returned as computed, -0.623541 at step 17. `clippedToZero` counts percentiles alone. Nothing holds a forecast at its last positive value, and a negative forecast is a result like any other.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 12, 'On the teaching run the P10 (high) less the P90 (low) is 51.569125 at step 1, 178.424168 at step 6 and 324.803671 at step 12. Why does the width grow with the step?', '["The engine adds one more month of forecast errors to the residual pool at every step", "Residuals are scaled up by the step number before they are drawn, as analytic intervals are", "Each path carries all its earlier draws through the state update, so the paths fan out", "The interval is a fixed share of the point forecast, and that share grows as the forecast falls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The simulated value updates the level and trend, so by step 6 each path holds six draws, each moving the state the next starts from, and the spread of the paths grows. The pool is fixed at the scored in-sample residuals. The engine gives no analytic interval and scales no residual. The width has no fixed ratio to the point forecast, which falls only from 204.969642 to 169.556510.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 13, 'At step 12 of the teaching run the width of the interval equals the P10 (high), 324.803671. What should a reader do before quoting that width in a decision?', '["Double the width, since the zero rule halves every interval it touches at a step", "Quote the width as printed, because the zero rule never changes a width, only the percentile it acts on", "Rerun with more paths until the P90 (low) climbs above 0 at that step, since more paths narrow the low tail", "Read the run with nonNegative false as well, since a P90 reported as 0 leaves the width measuring the high side alone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Once the low case is reported as 0 the width stops measuring the spread of the paths below the middle; reading the run with nonNegative false shows the simulated P90, and the course asks the reader to say which width is quoted. No rule halves or doubles anything. The zero rule does change the width here, which is the point. More paths steady the percentiles; at 10000 paths the P90 at step 12 is still 0.000000.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 14, '`fitSmoothing` accepts h 0 and answers with an empty forecast. What does `forecastIntervals` do when passed h 0?', '["Refuses it, naming `h`: \"h must be a whole number from 1 to 10000\"", "Refuses it with the fit''s message, \"h must be a whole number from 0 to 10000\", naming `h`", "Uses h 12, the horizon of the teaching bootstrap, whenever h is 0 or left out of the call", "Percentiles come back for step 0, which is the last month of the series itself"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bootstrap forecasts at least one step, so its h runs from 1, and h 0 is refused in the engine''s words quoted. The fit''s rule and message, from 0 to 10000, belong to a different function, and under them h 0 would be accepted. No default horizon of 12 exists; 12 is simply the horizon the course chose for its teaching run. Step 1 is the first month past the series; there is no step 0.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-percentiles-and-their-labels', 15, 'A second tool reads percentiles by linear interpolation between sorted values, the numpy default. What does the course advise before its P90 is set beside this engine''s?', '["Compare them directly, since every quantile rule gives one and the same value on 1000 sorted paths", "Check which quantile rule and which label convention it uses before comparing any figure", "Allow a tolerance of 1.00e-12 between the two, the band the engine keeps on percentiles", "Trust the numpy figure, since interpolation is exact where the platform rule rounds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The quantile and the P labels are both stated choices, each with an alternative in common use: linear interpolation, and P90 read as the 90th percentile. Either can move a figure, so both are checked first. Two rules can differ on the same sorted values. There is no band on a percentile; 1.00e-12 is the tie band of the grid and the ranking. The platform rule is exact on the sorted values, as interpolation is by its own definition.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 1, 'Where does `arpsForecast` get its decline curve from?', '["Its own least-squares Arps fit, written for monthly steps and checked against the decline curve engine", "A Holt fit on the logarithm of the rates, read back afterwards as the equivalent Arps curve", "fitArpsModel for the fit, and a hyperbolic formula of its own for the forecasts past the last month", "engines/dca/arps.js, whose fitArpsModel fits and calculateArpsHyperbolic forecasts, imported unchanged"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis names both imported functions, and nothing about Arps is re-implemented in the forecasting engine; the decline curve analysis course teaches the equations. There is no second fit to check against, no smoothing method stands in for Arps, and the forecasts come from calculateArpsHyperbolic, the decline curve engine''s own function.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 2, 'On EKENE-P1 the hyperbolic fit has RMSE 12.956885, the exponential 42.600547 and the harmonic 122.013020. Why does Auto-Select return Hyperbolic?', '["It keeps the model with the smallest Di, the gentlest decline of the three it fits", "Auto-Select keeps the lowest RMSE of the three models", "The hyperbolic model is the default whenever the fitted b lies between 0 and 1", "Auto-Select returns the model the well was generated from, which it reads from the series"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "Auto-Select takes the lowest RMSE", and the hyperbolic fit''s 12.956885 is lowest. The smallest Di on EKENE-P1 is the exponential''s 0.036564, so a Di rule would pick the exponential. No b range makes a model the default. The engine reads only the rates; it knows nothing of how the synthetic well was drawn.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 3, 'EKENE-P1''s Arps fit returns Di 0.060069. In what unit is that figure?', '["Per month, because month k of the series is passed to fitArpsModel as day k", "Per day, the unit in which fitArpsModel measures time on every fit it makes", "Per year, after the engine converts the fit''s days to a calendar before returning", "As an effective decline over the 48 months, a fraction that carries no time unit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis: "step k is passed as day k, so qi is per step and Di per step", and a step here is a month. fitArpsModel does work in days, but the days it is handed are months, so the figure is per month; the generator stated 0.06 per month for this well. The engine converts nothing, and a per-day, per-year or effective figure would be a different number.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 4, 'EKENE-P1''s fitted b prints 0.500000. A script tests the returned b for equality with 0.5. What does the test find?', '["Equal, since the grid runs from 0.05 by 0.05 to 2 and 0.5 is one of its points", "Equal, because the engine rounds every fitted b to six decimals before it hands the fit back to the caller", "Not equal: the b grid builds its values by adding steps in floating point, and this one is off by 5.55e-17", "Not equal, because 3 percent noise moved the fitted b off the generator''s stated 0.5"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course prints the difference before it says so: fitArpsModel''s b grid accumulates its steps in floating point, so a grid value is not the decimal it prints as, here by 5.55e-17. That the grid passes near 0.5 does not make the value exactly 0.5, and the engine returns b unrounded. The noise does not move b off a grid point; b is always one of the grid''s values.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 5, 'Auto-Select on EKENE-P2 uses 45 of its 48 months. What time index does month 25, the first after the shut-in, carry in the fit?', '["22, as the dropped months close up and every later month moves forward three", "0, since the fit restarts its clock at the first month after a shut-in ends", "25: later months keep their own index, so the gap stays in the time axis", "None: the first month after a shut-in is dropped too, as a restart transient"]'::jsonb, 2, 'fitArpsModel drops the three zero months, and the months after them are still passed as their own days, so month 25 stays at 25. Closing the gap would compress the decline in time. t = 0 sits at the first positive value of the whole series, which on EKENE-P2 is index 0. Only zero and negative rates are dropped: 48 less 3 is 45.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 6, 'Why does fitArpsModel drop zero rates before it fits?', '["Each model is a line through the rates on the log, reciprocal or q^-b scale, and none is defined at 0", "Zeros are outliers that the data quality course would remove, and the Arps fit removes them on that course''s behalf", "Dropping the zeros lifts R2, which is the figure Auto-Select uses to choose between the exponential, harmonic and hyperbolic", "The smoothing methods drop zero months too, so the two kinds of fit see the same months"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fits are least-squares lines on the log, reciprocal or q^-b scale, and a rate of 0 has no value on any of them, so shut-in months are dropped: "fitArpsModel drops zero and negative values (shut-in months) before fitting". A shut-in month is a real value. Auto-Select chooses by the lowest RMSE. The smoothing methods take a zero as a value and fit through it.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 7, 'Two months of 0 are put in front of EKENE-P1''s 48 months, which hold no zero of their own, and `arpsForecast` is run again. What does the result report about where time starts?', '["`t0Index` 0, with the two leading zeros fitted as the first two points of the decline, rates like any other", "A refusal naming `y`, because a series may not open on a zero rate", "qi read at index 0 and carried back across the zeros, with `t0Index` 2", "`t0Index` 2 and 2 months dropped, with `fitted` null before index 2 and qi the fitted rate at t = 0"]'::jsonb, 3, 't = 0 is the first positive value, and the basis says so: "t = 0 at index 2, the first positive value". The two zeros are dropped before the fit, and fitted values before index 2 are null. Leading zeros are accepted; the only positive-count rule is at least 3. qi is the fitted rate at t = 0, which is index 2 here, and nothing is carried back across the zeros. On a series with shut-in months of its own, such as EKENE-P2, the count dropped takes in those months as well.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 8, '`arpsForecast` is passed 5 months, two of them producing. What comes back?', '["A fit through the 2 producing months, with a warning that R2 is undefined on two points", "A refusal naming `y`: \"y has 2 positive values: fitArpsModel needs at least 3 (it drops zero and negative rates)\"", "Every zero is replaced by the smallest positive rate given, and all 5 months are fitted", "A refusal naming `y`: \"y has 2 values: fitArpsModel needs at least 3 positive values\""]'::jsonb, 1, 'Zeros are dropped before the fit, and fitArpsModel needs at least 3 positive values; the refusal names `y` in the words quoted. The boundary is 3 fitted and 2 refused, with no warning route. Nothing is filled or replaced: zeros are dropped. The message that counts values is the refusal for a series of two months; here 5 values were passed, so the message counts the positive ones.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 9, 'The rising series 100, 120, 150, 190, 240 is passed to `arpsForecast` with Auto-Select. Why is it refused?', '["Every model''s least-squares line slopes the wrong way, giving Di <= 0, so no fit has finite qi > 0 and Di > 0", "Auto-Select finds a harmonic fit with a negative Di and returns it anyway, flagged by an R2 that falls below 0 on those five months", "An exponential growth curve is fitted and returned, since the engine treats a rising well as an Arps case with Di below 0", "The series is shorter than the 6 months the Arps fit needs before any model is tried"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s words: "y gives no Arps fit: fitArpsModel found no exponential, harmonic or hyperbolic fit with finite qi > 0 and Di > 0 on the 5 positive values", and its parenthesis says a line that shows no decline gives Di <= 0. No fit with Di of 0 or below is returned, as a growth curve or otherwise. The least the fit accepts is 3 positive values, and 5 are here.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 10, 'In a comparison, the training window at one origin gives `fitArpsModel` only 2 positive months. What happens to the comparison?', '["Refused as a whole, naming `y`, as `arpsForecast` is on the same months", "Arps is ranked last, behind every smoothing method, with its metrics set to null", "The arps row takes the naive forecast at that origin, so every method keeps a figure", "The arps row carries an error and no metrics and is left unranked, while the smoothing methods are ranked"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a comparison the same condition leaves the arps row without numbers: on the stated series 900, 880, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 870, 860, 850, 845 from first origin 6, its error reads "at origin 6 the training window has 2 positive values: fitArpsModel needs at least 3 (it drops zero and negative rates)", and the smoothing methods rank ses, damped, holt. The call is not refused. An unranked method is never placed last. Nothing stands in for a failed fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 11, 'Auto-Select chooses Exponential on EKENE-P3, with b 0.000000 and RMSE 100.897804. What planted structure keeps one Arps curve from following this well?', '["Three months of shut-in at a rate of 0, with a workover lift in the months after it", "A facility-limited plateau held at 1500.000000 for months 0 to 8 before the decline", "Allocation noise of 12 percent on every month, which blurs the decline''s shape", "Only three months of production, too few for the hyperbolic grid to settle on a b"]'::jsonb, 1, 'EKENE-P3 holds exactly 1500.000000 for months 0 to 8 and declines from that rate after, and a single Arps curve declines from t = 0. The shut-in and workover are EKENE-P2''s, the 12 percent noise is EKENE-P4''s, and three months on production is EKENE-P6. The course states each well''s planted structure.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 12, 'EKENE-P4''s Arps fit has R2 0.898126, the lowest of the five long wells. Which reading does the course support?', '["EKENE-P4''s Arps baseline will score the worst MASE of the five long wells in any backtest the course could run", "The hyperbolic fit failed a check at that R2 and Auto-Select fell back to the exponential", "An Arps curve follows this noisy well less closely than the others, on months the fit has already seen", "An R2 below 0.9 makes the engine refuse to forecast the well past its last month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'R2 describes the fit on the months it was fitted to, and EKENE-P4 carries 12 percent allocation noise. Whether its baseline forecasts well is a question for a backtest on the same origins, and the course prints no such backtest comparing the five wells. Auto-Select returns Hyperbolic on EKENE-P4. No R2 threshold refuses a forecast.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 13, 'EKENE-P2''s Auto-Select fit returns b 0.950000, while the generator stated b 0.3 for its underlying decline. What does the course make of the gap?', '["The fit misread b because each month was passed to it as a day rather than a month", "The fit follows the whole record, lifted months included, so one curve is stretched over two stretches of history", "0.950000 is the top of the b grid, so the search simply stopped at its upper edge", "The three shut-in zeros were fitted as rates and pulled b up toward the harmonic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fit reads every positive month, the workover-lifted ones included, and finds the single curve that best follows all of them; a b this far from the stated one hints that one Arps curve does not describe the whole history. Passing a month as a day sets the unit of Di and has no bearing on b. The b grid runs to 2. The zeros are dropped before the fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 14, 'How does the course decide whether the Arps baseline forecasts a well better than a smoothing method?', '["R2 decides it: on EKENE-P1 the Arps fit''s 0.997703 already shows the baseline is the better forecast", "Compare the RMSE of each fit, which Auto-Select has minimised on the months before the last", "The step 12 forecasts decide, the lower of the two being the more cautious and so the better", "A backtest of both from the same origins, since an R2 scores only months the fit already saw"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Arps fit and its R2 describe months already seen; a forecast is judged on months it never saw, from the same origins for every method, which is what `compareWithArps` does. An in-sample RMSE has the same limit as R2. A lower forecast is no better for being lower; the metric on held-out months decides.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-arps-baseline', 15, 'EKENE-P1 fitted with `modelType` ''Harmonic'' returns b 1.000000 and RMSE 122.013020. What does naming a model do?', '["Fits that model alone and returns it, whatever its RMSE against the other two", "Fits all three models and returns the named one only when its RMSE is the lowest", "Refuses the call unless the named model is the one Auto-Select would have chosen", "Fixes b at the named model''s value, then fits qi and Di by Auto-Select''s own rule"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Auto-Select, the default, takes the lowest RMSE; naming a model fits that one alone, and the harmonic''s RMSE is the highest of the three here. Nothing is compared or refused: only a name the engine does not offer is refused, in the words "modelType must be ''Auto-Select'', ''Exponential'', ''Harmonic'' or ''Hyperbolic''". qi and Di are the named model''s own least-squares fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 1, 'A comparison on EKENE-P2''s 48 months states its origins as "28, 28 + 3, ... while o + 6 <= 48". Which origins does every method share?', '["28, 31, 34, 37 and 40", "28, 31, 34, 37, 40 and 43, the last with a shorter horizon", "28, 34 and 40, one every six months", "Every month from 28 to 42, since horizon 6 fits each one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Origins start at 28 and step by 3 while o + 6 <= 48, which gives 28, 31, 34, 37 and 40; the next, 43, would need months 43 to 48 and there is no month 48. No origin runs with a shorter horizon. A step of 6 would give 28, 34 and 40, and a step of 1 every month to 42; this comparison''s step is 3.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 2, 'The holt row of the EKENE-P2 comparison from first origin 28, horizon 6, step 3 has MASE 0.571171. What does a plain `backtest` of holt from the same first origin, horizon and step return?', '["A lower MASE, since the comparison holds parameters where the backtest refits them at each origin", "A different MASE, since the comparison scales each error by the Arps fit''s error at that origin", "Exactly 0.571171, because the comparison''s smoothing rows are backtests run side by side", "0.605968, because `backtest` scores holt against the Arps baseline''s forecasts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course checks it: the holt row''s MASE is exactly `backtest`''s on the same origins. Both refit by default. The comparison''s MASE scales each error by its own origin''s in-sample naive error, as a backtest does; no Arps error enters it. 0.605968 is the arps row''s own MASE in the comparison.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 3, 'A comparison is run with `refit` false. What happens to the Arps baseline at each origin?', '["Held at the parameters fitted on the first window, exactly as the smoothing methods are held at every later origin", "It is refitted on every training window anyway, while the smoothing methods hold their first window''s parameters", "Dropped from the comparison, since a baseline fitted only once cannot be ranked fairly against the other three rows", "It is fitted once on the whole series, because the Arps fit has no refit setting of its own at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "engines/dca/arps.js fitArpsModel refitted on each training window", and Arps is refitted whatever `refit` says, so a forecast note on such a run says the baseline was refitted regardless. Holding Arps is what the smoothing methods do with refit false, and the baseline does not follow them. It stays in the comparison. Fitting it on the whole series would put the scored months into its fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 4, 'The EKENE-P2 comparison from first origin 28 ranks holt, damped, arps, ses by MASE. Ranked by sMAPE instead, which method comes first?', '["holt, as it does by every metric the engine offers on this comparison", "arps, whose sMAPE of 10.094073 is the lowest of the four rows", "ses, since sMAPE favours a flat forecast on a well that has just been lifted", "damped, whose sMAPE of 9.770766 is the lowest of the four"]'::jsonb, 3, 'By sMAPE the ranking is damped, arps, holt, ses: damped''s 9.770766 is the lowest. holt ranks first by MAE, RMSE and MASE and third by sMAPE, which is why a ranking is quoted with its metric. arps''s 10.094073 is second lowest. ses is last on every metric here, sMAPE included.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 5, 'What reason does the engine state for ranking by MASE when `rankBy` is left out?', '["MASE is the only metric the engine computes for the Arps row", "It is in bbl/d, so its ranking reads as a rate", "Ranking by MASE puts the Arps baseline last, which makes any smoothing win clear", "MASE compares wells of any size and stays defined through a shut-in"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conventions table gives the reason: MASE compares wells of any size and stays defined through a shut-in, where MAPE is null. The Arps row carries MAE, RMSE, MAPE and sMAPE as well. MASE has no unit; MAE and RMSE are the metrics in bbl/d. MASE puts arps first on EKENE-P1, so it favours no method.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 6, 'A comparison is asked to rank by `rankBy` ''me'', the mean error. What comes back?', '["Mean error ranks it, lowest first, which puts the most negative bias at the top", "A refusal naming `rankBy`: \"rankBy must be ''mae'', ''rmse'', ''mape'', ''smape'' or ''mase''\"", "A ranking by MASE, the default, with a warning that the name ''me'' was not recognised by the engine", "A refusal naming `methods`: \"methods must be a non-empty array of ''ses'', ''holt'' and ''damped''\""]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ranking takes five names, and anything else is refused with the field named, in the words quoted. The engine never ranks by the mean error, signed or unsigned, and it never falls back to a default with a warning; a name it does not offer is refused before any fit. The methods message is for an empty list, and nothing was wrong with the methods here.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 7, 'The golden `cmp-constant-ties` is a constant 50 for 10 months, with the methods listed damped, ses, holt and ranked by mae. What ranking comes back?', '["ses, holt, damped, the order in which the engine defines its methods", "holt, damped, ses, arps, all four tied at an MAE of 0", "The listed order, damped, ses, holt; arps is unranked", "arps, damped, ses, holt, as Arps forecasts a constant exactly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every smoothing method forecasts a constant exactly, so each MAE is 0 and the three tie; inside the band a tie keeps the listed order, damped, ses, holt. Arps cannot fit a flat series, so its row carries an error and it is unranked. The engine''s own order of names plays no part, and arps never ties in here because it has no metric.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 8, 'How close must two metrics be for a ranking to treat them as tied?', '["Within 1.00e-12 of each other, relative, and then the listed order decides with arps last", "Equal at six decimals, the precision at which the course prints every metric in its tables", "Within 1.00e-9 of each other, the band to which the damped forecast''s limit is checked", "Equal to the last bit, since any difference at all, however small, settles the order"]'::jsonb, 0, '`RANK_TIE_REL` is 1.00e-12, and the ranking basis reads "values within 1e-12 (relative) keep the listed order (methods as given, arps last)". Two metrics printing alike at six decimals can still differ by more than the band, and then the smaller ranks first. 1.00e-9 is a check the course applies to the damped limit, a different thing. A last-bit rule would let rounding decide, which the band exists to avoid.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 9, 'EKENE-P1 is compared from first origin 30, horizon 6, step 3 with the methods listed damped, holt, ses. What is the ranking by MASE?', '["damped, holt, ses, arps, which is the listed order with arps put last", "arps, ses, holt, damped, since arps leads and the rest keep the listing reversed", "arps, damped, holt, ses, the same as with the default listing", "No ranking, as EKENE-P1 ties with an Arps curve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without a tie the metric decides and the listing changes nothing: arps 0.199862, damped 0.287529, holt 0.346480, ses 1.067547. The listed order only settles metrics within 1.00e-12 relative. No rule reverses a listing. How the synthetic well was drawn explains why arps wins; it does not stop the ranking.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 10, 'The methods passed to `compareWithArps` are ses, holt, ses. What happens?', '["It ranks ses once and drops the repeat without a message, since both copies would score the same", "It is refused, in the engine''s words \"methods[2] repeats ses\", the field naming the third method counted from 0", "A refusal naming `methods[1]`: \"methods[1] must be ''ses'', ''holt'' or ''damped''\"", "Both copies of ses are ranked, and they tie within the band and so keep their listed order"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A method listed twice is refused, and the field names its position counted from 0, so `methods[2]` is the third method listed. Nothing is dropped quietly, and holt, in second place, is a method the engine offers, so nothing is refused at `methods[1]`. A repeated method is not ranked twice; it never reaches the ranking.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 11, 'EKENE-P2 is compared from first origin 12, horizon 6, step 3, ranked by MAPE. The shut-in months fall in the actuals of some origins. What comes back?', '["Refused, naming `rankBy`, since MAPE cannot be computed anywhere on this well", "MASE, the default, takes over, and the engine ranks by it whenever MAPE is null", "A ranking by MAPE over the origins that avoid the shut-in, with the rest dropped", "An empty `ranking`, `best` null, and ses, holt, damped and arps all listed in `unranked`"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every method''s pooled MAPE is null, because MAPE divides by each actual and a shut-in month is 0, so nothing is left to rank: the ranking is empty, best is null and all four are unranked. A null metric is a result the engine returns with its reason. The engine does not switch metric; ranked by MASE the same call ranks all four. Pooled metrics average every origin, and no origin is dropped.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 12, 'How does a forecast note report a method that a comparison left unranked?', '["As unranked, with the reason from `notes` or the row''s error, and never placed last by hand", "Last in the ranking, since a method with no figure cannot be said to have beaten any of the others", "It is left out of the note, as the engine treats an unranked method as a run that failed", "By whichever other metric it does have, so that every method keeps a place in the order"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An unranked method has not lost; it has no figure on that metric, for a reason the engine states, and the note gives the reason. Placing it last invents a comparison that was not made. A note leaves out no metric the engine could not give; it writes it with its reason. Mixing metrics in one order ranks by two yardsticks at once.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 13, 'On EKENE-P1 from first origin 30, horizon 6, step 3, the Arps baseline ranks first at MASE 0.199862. What must a note that leans on that win also say?', '["An Arps baseline beats exponential smoothing on any declining well in the field", "That the well was drawn from an Arps curve, so the win is partly by construction on a synthetic field", "damped, at 0.287529, sits within the tie band of arps and so shares first place", "The note must add that the smoothing methods held their first window''s parameters"]'::jsonb, 1, 'EKENE-P1 was drawn from an Arps curve (stated), and a note that leans on the win says the well was built that way, because a real well need not follow a single Arps curve. After EKENE-P2''s workover a smoothing method wins, so no rule says Arps wins on declining wells. 0.287529 is far outside 1.00e-12 relative of 0.199862. That comparison ran with refit true, the default.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 14, 'After EKENE-P2''s workover, from origins 28 to 40, the arps row has a mean error of -23.147528 bbl/d and holt 20.751043. What do the signs say?', '["Arps forecasts low and holt high, because a negative error means the forecast fell short of the rate", "Both are unbiased overall, since mean errors of opposite sign cancel out across the methods", "Arps forecasts the well high and holt forecasts it low, since an error is actual minus forecast", "Arps did better on this well, because its mean error is the one further below zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An error is actual minus forecast, so a negative mean error means the forecast was above the actuals: the Arps fit, pulled by the months before the uplift, forecasts high, and holt low. A negative error means the forecast was high. Errors of two different methods never cancel each other. Being further from zero is more bias; by MASE holt ranks first here and arps third.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-ranking-methods-against-arps', 15, 'EKENE-P2 compared from first origins 26, 28, 30 and 32 (horizon 6, step 3) puts holt, holt, damped and holt first by MASE. What does the course draw from the four?', '["A ranking is reported with its origins, horizon, step and metric, since the winner moves with them", "holt is the method to use on EKENE-P2, since it ranks first from three of the four first origins tried", "The run from origin 30 must be in error, since damped cannot rank above holt after a workover", "Arps can be ruled out on EKENE-P2 for good, as it never ranks higher than third from any origin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A smoothing method ranks first from every one of the four, and which one changes with the origins, so a ranking is one test and travels with its origins, horizon, step and metric. Three wins of four is still a count of tests on one well. From origin 30 damped ranks first at 0.461235, a result like the others. From origin 26 arps ranks second.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 1, 'A caller gives alpha by hand to `fitSmoothing`. Where does the accepted range begin and end?', '["Numbers strictly between 0 and 1, since alpha 0 would leave the level stuck at y_1", "0 to 1 when given and 0.8 to 0.98 when fitted, the box the search keeps it in", "Any number from 0 to 1 with both ends included, while -0.1 and 1.2 are refused", "Any number at all, clipped into the box from 0 to 1 before the fit starts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The boundary table: alpha and beta given, 0 and 1 accepted (inclusive), -0.1 and 1.2 refused with "alpha must be a number from 0 to 1 (inclusive)". alpha 0 is legal: the level then never moves from y_1, and the forecast is month 0''s rate for ever. 0.8 to 0.98 is the fitted box for phi, and a fitted alpha stays in 0 to 1. A given value outside the range is refused outright.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 2, 'A phi given to `fitSmoothing` is checked against which rule?', '["From 0.8 to 0.98, the same box the fit searches when phi is left free", "From 0 to 1 with both ends included, the same rule as alpha and beta", "Above 0 and below 1, since a phi of 1 would turn the damped method into holt", "Above 0 and at most 1: 0.01 and 1 are accepted, 0 and 1.05 refused"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A given phi may be any value above 0 and at most 1, exclusive at 0 and inclusive at 1; the refusal says so, adding that a fitted phi is searched from 0.8 to 0.98. The fitted box is narrower than the given rule; phi 0.5 can be given and held. A phi of 0 is refused. phi 1 is accepted, and damped with phi 1 returns the same SSE and forecasts as holt, bit for bit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 3, 'h 0 is accepted by some functions and refused by another. Which way round is it?', '["`fitSmoothing` and `arpsForecast` accept it, and `forecastIntervals` refuses it", "`forecastIntervals` accepts it, and `fitSmoothing` refuses it as an empty horizon", "All three accept it, since 0 is the default h in every function that takes one", "`arpsForecast` refuses it, because an Arps curve needs at least one step to draw"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For a fit and an Arps forecast h runs from 0 to 10000, and h 0 returns an empty forecast; the bootstrap forecasts at least one step, so its h runs from 1, and h 0 is refused with "h must be a whole number from 1 to 10000". h is 0 when left out of a fit, while the bootstrap has its own rule. `arpsForecast` shares the fit''s rule.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 4, 'A backtest on 48 months with horizon 6: which is the largest first origin it accepts?', '["41, since the last origin must leave one month spare after its horizon", "42, since o + horizon = n is accepted, and 43 is refused", "43, because a horizon may run past the series and score what actuals remain", "48, the series length itself, where the origin forecasts months beyond the data"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The last origin must leave all its actuals inside the series: o + horizon = n is accepted and o + horizon > n refused, so 42 is accepted (42 + 6 = 48). No spare month is needed. An origin whose horizon runs past the end would be scored on fewer than 6 actuals, and the refusal says so: "an origin above 42 leaves fewer than 6 actuals".'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 5, '`compareWithArps` is passed ses alone, with first origin 2. What comes back?', '["An accepted comparison, since ses needs only 2 training values and is the only method listed", "A comparison with the arps row unranked at origin 2 and ses ranked alone", "A refusal, because ses cannot be compared without holt or damped beside it", "A refusal: a comparison needs 3 training values whatever methods are listed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words: "firstOrigin must be a whole number from 3 to 42 (the comparison needs 3 training values; an origin above 42 leaves fewer than 6 actuals)". The boundary table says 2 is refused even for ses alone; the comparison''s rule is its own and stricter than a backtest of ses, which accepts 2. The call is refused, so no row is scored. A list of one method is accepted.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 6, 'With `nonNegative` true, a percentile at some step comes out exactly 0 as simulated. How is it reported?', '["As 0, reported as simulated and left out of the `clippedToZero` count", "As 0, and counted in `clippedToZero` along with every negative percentile", "As null, since a rate of exactly zero is read as a shut-in month", "Raised to the smallest positive simulated value at that step"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The boundary is per rule: a percentile at or above 0 is reported as simulated, and only one below 0 is reported as 0 and counted. So a percentile of exactly 0 is left alone and not counted. The engine never turns a percentile into null, and no rule raises one to another simulated value.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 7, 'During the compass search, a trial of +step would carry a fitted alpha to 1.05. What does the engine do with that trial?', '["Refuses the fit, naming `alpha`, since an alpha above 1 is refused when it is given", "Evaluates alpha 1.05 as it stands, since the box binds only the points of the grid", "Clips it to 1, the edge of the box, and skips the trial if the clip leaves alpha where it was", "Reflects the trial back inside the box to 0.95 and evaluates the SSE there"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A compass trial past an edge is clipped to it, and a trial the clip leaves where it was is skipped; a fitted alpha stays in [0, 1]. A refusal is for a value the caller gives; a search trial is only clipped. The box binds the search as well as the grid. No trial is reflected.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 8, 'EKENE-P4''s damped fit lists `atBounds` phi = 0.8. How does the course read that?', '["The fit failed, and the engine would have refused it had the search not converged", "phi is a fitted value on the lower edge of its box, the strongest damping the fit may choose", "phi was given as 0.8 and held fixed, and `atBounds` is where a given value is recorded", "The search ran out of evaluations at the edge and returned a warning with the fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A parameter on its bound is a fitted value like any other; it says the SSE was still falling at the edge of the box, and for phi at 0.8 that is the strongest damping allowed. The fit converged, and nothing is refused. A given parameter is listed in `fixed` and never in `atBounds`, as phi 0.5 given on EKENE-P1 shows. No fit in the course reaches the evaluation cap.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 9, 'Two trials in one compass sweep lower the SSE by exactly the same amount. Which one does the search move to?', '["The earlier: +step before -step, alpha before beta before phi", "The later one, since each new trial replaces the best whenever it is no worse", "Whichever lies within 1.00e-12 of the best grid point''s SSE", "Neither, since a tie counts as no improvement and the step is halved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tie rules table: in the compass search a trial must be strictly below the current SSE to replace it, so the earlier trial is kept, in the stated order. Replacing on no worse would let the later trial win. 1.00e-12 is the band of the grid and of the ranking, and it plays no part here. Both trials improve on the current SSE, so the sweep has improved and the step is not halved.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 10, 'In the golden `holt-linear-exact`, the line 10, 12, 14, ..., 24, every alpha and beta give SSE 0. What does the fit return?', '["alpha 1 and beta 1, the naive forecast of a line, which the grid reaches at its very last point", "A refusal, since parameters that cannot be identified cannot be fitted at all", "alpha 0.5 and beta 0.5, the middle of the box, which the engine takes when every point ties", "alpha 0 and beta 0, the grid''s first point, kept by the tie rule, with 0 moves in the search"]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the coarse grid a later point replaces the best only when its SSE is below best x (1 - 1.00e-12), so with every point at 0 the first, alpha 0 and beta 0, is kept, and the compass search makes 0 moves. The parameters are not identified, and the tie rule is what makes the answer the same every time; nothing is refused. The last grid point never displaces a tie, and no middle point is chosen.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 11, 'Holt on EKENE-P1 ends its compass search at a final step of 7.45e-10 after 26 halvings. What stopped it?', '["It reached the cap of 200000 SSE evaluations before the step could fall any further", "A sweep at a step of at most 2^-30 of the range, 9.31e-10, improved nothing", "The SSE fell below the grid''s tie band of 1.00e-12, so no further move could count", "Its parameters stopped changing at six decimals, the precision the course prints them at"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stop rule is a rule on the step: the step starts at 0.05 of each range, halves after a sweep with no improvement, and the search stops when a sweep at a step of at most 2^-30 of the range improves nothing; `converged` is true. Holt on EKENE-P1 takes 329 evaluations, far from the cap. 1.00e-12 is a relative tie band on the grid and puts no floor under the SSE. Printed decimals play no part in the search.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 12, 'What does `fitSmoothing` return if its search reaches 200000 SSE evaluations before the step falls to 2^-30 of the range?', '["A refusal naming `y`, since a series that long cannot be fitted within the cap", "It restarts from the next best grid point with a fresh budget of evaluations", "A fit with `converged` false and a warning, its parameters being where the search stopped", "The grid''s best point, with every compass move discarded as unconverged"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The evaluation cap warns and never refuses: the fit returns with `converged` false and a message in `warnings`, and its parameters are read as the point where the search stopped, which may not be the minimum. No restart and no discarding happen. No fit in this course reaches the cap; the three EKENE-P1 fits take 38, 329 and 1067 evaluations.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 13, 'EKENE-P6, with 3 months, is backtested with holt at horizon 1. What does the engine reply?', '["\"y has 3 values: a backtest with horizon 1 needs at least 4 (''holt'' needs 3 training values, then 1 actual)\"", "A backtest from origin 3 with no actuals to score, returning every one of its metrics as null", "An accepted backtest from first origin 2, since horizon 1 needs only a single actual after it", "\"horizon must be a whole number, 1 or more\", naming `horizon`, since 1 is below the least horizon"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A backtest needs the method''s fitting length, 3 for holt, plus a full horizon of actuals, so 3 values are one short and the refusal names `y` in the words quoted. An origin with no actuals is never run. holt from origin 2 is refused, because holt needs 3 training values. Horizon 1 is legal; the refusal is for a horizon of 0.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 14, 'EKENE-P1 repeated to 5005 values is backtested with step 1, and the engine refuses, naming `step`. What remedy does its message name?', '["Shorten the series to fewer than 5000 values, since that is where the series cap sits", "Lower the horizon to 1, since each origin is charged for every step of its horizon", "Call `compareWithArps` instead, whose own cap on origins is ten times higher", "Raise step or firstOrigin, since 5003 origins are above the 5000 a backtest accepts"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words: "step gives 5003 origins, above the 5000 a backtest accepts: raise step or firstOrigin". The series cap is 100000 values, far above 5005. The origin count depends on the first origin and the step; the horizon only moves the last origin. A comparison shares the cap of 5000 origins.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-bounds-and-caps', 15, 'A series of 100001 values reaches the engine. Which functions refuse it?', '["Only `backtest` and `compareWithArps`, which fit once per origin", "All six, while 100000 values are fitted by `fitSmoothing`", "None: the only length cap is on h, which stops at 10000", "`forecastIntervals` alone, as 1000 paths on that series are too many"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The boundary table: 100000 values fitted by `fitSmoothing`, 100001 refused by all six, with "y has 100001 values, above the 100000 this engine accepts". The origin cap is a separate rule. h has its own cap of 10000, and the series has its own of 100000. The path cap is on nSims, 100000, and it has no link to the series length.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 1, 'The engine writes its recursions in component form, with beta the trend weight on the level change. What reason does it state for that choice?', '["Most texts multiply beta by alpha, and the engine follows the majority of them", "beta is statsmodels'' smoothing_trend, so a parameter compares directly", "Component form runs faster, and speed is what the engine chose its form by", "The error-correction form has no way to express a damped method at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conventions table gives the reason: "beta is statsmodels'' smoothing_trend, so a parameter compares directly". Beta multiplied by alpha is the alternative some texts use, and the engine did not take it. Speed is not the stated reason. The error-correction form is the other alternative named, and the course makes no claim that it cannot damp.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 2, 'The engine starts at l_1 = y_1 and b_1 = y_2 - y_1, leaving y_2 unscored, where statsmodels estimates its initial states. What reason does the engine give?', '["A learner can start the recursion by hand", "Estimated initial states need at least 12 months of data, and new wells have fewer", "Starting at the first observation gives the lowest SSE that any start can reach", "NIST''s double smoothing example starts exactly this way, and the engine follows it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stated reason is that a learner can start the recursion by hand. No month count is attached to estimated starts. Nothing makes this start the lowest SSE: on EKENE-P1 at alpha 0.3 an initialLevel of 1150.000000 lowers the SSE from 312538.074209 to 297403.122315. The conventions table lists a mean of early differences as NIST double smoothing''s start, and the course''s NIST check gives b_1 = 0.8 as an initialTrend.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 3, 'What reason does the engine state for fitting by least one-step SSE, with a grid and then a compass search in a stated box?', '["A sure route to the global minimum, which a gradient optimiser cannot promise", "Speed: it runs faster than maximum likelihood on a series of 48 monthly rates", "It reproduces the parameters statsmodels returns, to six decimals, on every well", "Determinism: the same series gives the same parameters"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conventions table: "deterministic: the same series gives the same parameters", with stated tie rules, against maximum likelihood with a gradient optimiser as the alternative. The course claims no global minimum: two fits stopping at SSEs that print alike are the same minimum only as far as the search could tell. Speed is not the stated reason. statsmodels estimates its initial states, so its parameters can differ.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 4, 'Why does the engine search a fitted phi only from 0.8 to 0.98?', '["Below 0.8 a damped forecast turns negative on any declining well in the field it is fitted to", "Above 0.98 the engine refuses phi, since the method would then be holt", "FPP3 restricts an estimated phi to that range, and a phi outside it can still be given", "The Ekene wells were generated with a phi inside that range, and the box matches it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stated reason is FPP3 8.2''s restriction on an estimated phi; a given phi may be any value above 0 and at most 1, and phi 0.5 given on EKENE-P1 is accepted. A damped forecast levels off toward a limit, and nothing makes it negative below 0.8. phi 1 given is accepted, and it is holt. The wells were drawn from Arps curves, which carry no phi.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 5, 'Another tool drops a shut-in month before it computes MAPE. What does this engine do with the same month, and why?', '["Returns MAPE as null with the reason, since a shut-in month is real and dropping it changes the metric", "It drops the month too, and adds a note stating how many months of actuals were dropped", "Divides by a tiny number in place of the zero, so that MAPE stays finite and can be ranked", "Refuses the call, naming `actual`, until the shut-in month is filled or removed by the caller"]'::jsonb, 0, 'MAPE is null when any actual is 0, with the reason in `notes`, and the stated reason is that a shut-in month is real and dropping it changes the metric. Dropping the month and dividing by a tiny number are the two alternatives the conventions table names. A null metric is a result, and the other metrics are still numbers.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 6, 'Why does the engine take the MASE scale from the training months?', '["Training months never contain a flat stretch, so a scale taken from them can never come out as 0", "A seasonal lag of 12 is the default, and only the training months are sure to hold a full year of rates", "The scale is then fixed before the forecast is scored, so the months being forecast cannot move it", "A scale from the forecast months would turn MASE into a percentage error"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stated reason: "the scale is fixed before the forecast is scored". EKENE-P3''s plateau shows a training scale can be 0, and then MASE is null with its reason. The default lag m is 1, the month before. A scale from the forecast months is the out-of-sample naive error, the alternative named; it still gives a ratio with no unit, only a different one.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 7, 'Why does the backtest window expand from month 0, where a sliding window would keep a fixed length?', '["A sliding window cannot be refitted at each origin, while an expanding one can", "Only the early months carry EKENE-P3''s plateau, which every fit must see", "An expanding window gives every origin the same number of training months", "Every month before the origin is information a forecaster would have"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conventions table: "every month before the origin is information a forecaster would have". A sliding window can be refitted as easily; it forgets the early months on purpose. The plateau is one well''s structure and no reason for a rule on all of them. An expanding window grows by the step at each origin, so the training length differs: origin 24 trains on 24 months, origin 42 on 42.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 8, 'Which of these is the engine''s own convention for an interval?', '["Analytic intervals built from the variance of the one-step errors", "A residual bootstrap, residuals drawn as fitted, parameters held", "A centred bootstrap that subtracts the residual mean before each draw", "A bootstrap that refits the parameters on each simulated path"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conventions table: residual bootstrap, residuals as fitted without centring, parameters held; the reason is that no distribution is assumed and the method''s own errors are replayed. Analytic intervals, a centred bootstrap and a bootstrap that also resamples parameters are the three alternatives it names, and the engine builds none of them.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 9, 'A manager wants a twelve-month forecast of EKENE-P4 that responds to a planned choke change. What does the course say about this engine?', '["Pass the choke setting to `fitSmoothing` as a second series beside the rates of the well", "Use damped with phi fitted, since the damping adjusts the forecast to changes in choke", "The Arps baseline answers it, because the decline curve engine folds the choke into Di", "It runs no regression of rate on other variables such as choke; that work belongs to the machine learning course"]'::jsonb, 3, 'What is not built: no regression of rate on other variables such as choke, pressure or water cut; the machine learning course regresses. A forecasting method here reads only the series itself, so no second series can be passed. phi flattens the smoothed trend and knows nothing of a choke. The Arps fit reads the rates alone, and its Di is a decline per month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 10, 'Which of these can this engine return?', '["A rate at each future step, from a smoothing method or the Arps baseline", "An EUR for the well, from the Arps baseline fitted to all of its months", "A seasonal forecast by Holt-Winters for a well with a yearly cycle", "An analytic prediction interval around a damped point forecast"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine forecasts a rate at each future step and that is all a forecast from it claims. It computes no EUR, reserves or cumulative (the decline curve analysis course), builds no seasonal smoothing, and gives no analytic prediction interval: intervals come from the residual bootstrap alone.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 11, 'EKENE-P1 is passed with month 5 set to null. What does the engine do?', '["Fills month 5 by interpolating between months 4 and 6, and notes the filled month in the result", "Refuses the series as a whole: \"y must be an array of numbers\", since one null spoils every value", "It refuses by name at the first null index: \"y[5] must be a finite number: fill or drop missing values first\"", "Treats month 5 as a shut-in at rate 0, which the smoothing methods fit through like any value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A missing month is refused by name at the first index it meets, counting from 0, and nothing is filled; filling or dropping it is the caller''s decision, and the data quality course conditions rates. Interpolating and dropping are both caller decisions the engine will not make. The array message is for something passed in place of a series, and here the series was an array with one null in it. A shut-in is a real value of 0; a null is a missing one, and the engine does not guess which a null was.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 12, 'The engine''s exports are listed in full. How many names are there, and which one draws the bootstrap?', '["Six functions and no defaults, the bootstrap being `bootstrap`", "Seven, and `forecastIntervals` is the bootstrap", "Seven, with `backtest` returning the intervals as well", "Eight, one of them a Holt-Winters function named `seasonal`"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exported names, in full: DEFAULTS, accuracy, arpsForecast, backtest, compareWithArps, fitSmoothing, forecastIntervals. That is six functions and the DEFAULTS, seven names, and the intervals come from `forecastIntervals`. No function is called `bootstrap`. `backtest` returns origins, forecasts, errors and metrics, and no intervals. No seasonal smoothing is built.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 13, 'A forecast note quotes an interval from the bootstrap. Which conditions travel with the figure?', '["The seed alone, as a seed fixes every draw whatever the number of paths", "The method, nSims, the seed and nonNegative, with the P90 read as the low case", "nSims and the tolerance band of 1.00e-12 that the engine keeps on a percentile", "The R2 of the fit and the number of residuals trimmed from the pool before drawing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The note names the intervals with method, nSims, seed and nonNegative, and reads the P90 as the low case. The seed alone is not enough: the same seed at 100 and 1000 paths gives different percentiles. There is no band on a percentile; 1.00e-12 is the tie band of the grid and the ranking. R2 belongs to the Arps fit, and no residual is ever trimmed.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 14, 'The course''s note on EKENE-P1 ranks, by MASE from first origin 30, arps 0.199862, damped 0.287529, holt 0.346480 and ses 1.067547. What finding does the note end on?', '["The Arps baseline ranks first, and the damped method has not earned its place on EKENE-P1", "damped is the forecast to use on EKENE-P1, as it is the best of the three smoothing methods", "Every method but ses ties, since each MASE except ses''s lies below 1", "ses should be dropped from the note, leaving holt and damped reported side by side"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A smoothing method that cannot beat the Arps baseline on a well has not earned its place on that well, and the note says so; damped ranks second. Being the best smoothing method is not beating the baseline. MASE below 1 compares each method with the in-sample naive error; it creates no tie, and the tie band is 1.00e-12 relative. The note keeps every row, the baseline''s and ses''s included.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 15, 'A figure from this engine disagrees with one from another tool on the same series. What does the course say to do before comparing the numbers?', '["Take this engine''s figure, since its fits are deterministic and the other tool''s may not be", "Average the two figures, which cancels out the differences between the two tools", "Rerun with more paths or a finer grid until the two figures come to agree", "List the conventions each tool used, since both figures may be right under their own"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every convention is a stated choice with a real alternative in common use, and two figures that disagree may both be right under their own conventions; a comparison that does not name them compares the conventions without saying so. Determinism makes a figure reproducible, and it says nothing about which convention the other tool took. Averaging or rerunning hides the difference without explaining it.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Two analysts on different machines run `forecastIntervals` with damped on EKENE-P1, h 12, 1000 paths and seed 11. How do their percentiles compare?', '["Within about 1.00e-12 of each other, the band the engine keeps on every percentile it reports to a caller", "Agreeing to six decimals only, since each machine rounds its floating point arithmetic in its own way", "They are identical bit for bit, since every draw comes from one mulberry32 stream on that seed", "They differ, because the engine mixes the machine clock into the stream along with the seed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One seeded stream, drawn path by path and step by step, returns the same percentiles bit for bit for the same call; a figure named with its seed and nSims has one right value. There is no band on a percentile, and 1.00e-12 is the grid and ranking tie band. The course claims more than six-decimal agreement for the same call. No clock enters the stream.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Each draw of the teaching bootstrap picks residual number floor(u x 46) from the pool. What makes every residual equally likely to be picked?', '["u from mulberry32 lies from 0 up to 1, so floor(u x 46) runs evenly over the 46 positions", "The residuals are sorted first, and u picks a rank, so the middle ones come up most often", "Each residual is weighted by its size, so the draw leans toward the largest errors of the fit", "The pool is shuffled once per path, and each step takes the next residual without replacement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis: each step adds a residual drawn with replacement, index floor(u x 46), u from the seeded stream; with u from 0 up to 1 every index is equally likely, and the same residual can be picked again. Nothing is sorted or weighted before the draw. Drawing without replacement is a different scheme; this one is with replacement.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'Which single path of the teaching bootstrap would reproduce the damped point forecast at every step?', '["The path whose rate at step 12 is the P50 of the thousand, since the median path is the forecast", "The first path of the stream, which the engine runs with no residuals to set the point forecast", "No path; the point forecast is the mean of all 1000 paths at each step, which no one path follows", "One that drew a residual of exactly 0 at every step, so its state followed the fitted recursion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The point forecast comes from the fitted final state with nothing added, which is what a path adding 0 at every step would compute, since the simulated value then equals each one-step forecast. The P50 is a percentile read step by step, and on this run it differs from the point forecast at every step shown. The first path draws residuals like the rest. The engine returns no mean of the paths.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'ses on EKENE-P1, seed 11, 1000 paths, nonNegative false: at step 1 the paths run from P90 (low) 157.500000 to P10 (high) 213.200000 around a point forecast of 211.400000. What does the point forecast''s position show already at step 1?', '["It sits in the middle, as a first-step interval is always centred on the point forecast", "It sits near the top of the interval, because most residuals of this fit are negative month-to-month changes", "Nothing yet; the residuals only start to lean once the state has been updated at step 2", "The labels are swapped at step 1, since the high case must lie below the point forecast"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With alpha at 1 each ses residual is the change from the month before, mostly a fall on this decline, so one draw already pulls most paths below the last rate: the P50 at step 1 is 198.900000, under the point forecast of 211.400000. Residuals drawn as fitted are not centred, so no interval is centred by rule. The lean acts at the first draw. P10 (high) above P90 (low) is the right order, and nothing requires the high case below the forecast.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'When does the course say a caller should turn `nonNegative` false?', '["When the well can produce at a negative rate, so the paths are allowed to follow it", "To see how far below zero the paths went, or to compare with a tool that does not clip", "Whenever the point forecast goes below zero, so that the point forecast is clipped instead", "Every time, because a figure reported as 0 may never appear in a report of rates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The switch changes only the reported percentiles, and the course names its use: to see a percentile as simulated, or to match a tool that does not clip; for a report of rates it is left true, with the count reported as 0 stated. No well produces a negative rate. The point forecast is never clipped either way. A reported 0 may go in a report, with `clippedToZero` beside it.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Holt on EKENE-P5, h 12, seed 11, 1000 paths reports `clippedToZero` 11. What does the 11 count?', '["Paths that ended below zero at step 12, out of the 1000 the run simulated for the interval", "Steps at which holt''s own point forecast fell below zero and was then reported as 0 in the result", "Percentiles that were below 0 and are reported as 0, out of the 36 the run returns", "Months of EKENE-P5 whose rate lies below the last month''s 11.100000 bbl/d"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The count is of percentiles: three per step over 12 steps is 36, and 11 of them were negative and are reported as 0. The rule acts on percentiles, so no path is counted or changed. Holt''s point forecast stays above zero over these 12 steps, 2.887394 at step 12, and it is never clipped. The count has nothing to do with the series'' own months.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Which of the three P labels reads the same whether a reader takes it by exceedance or as a plain percentile?', '["P50, the median of the paths at each step", "P90, since 90 percent is symmetric about the middle of the paths", "P10, once nonNegative has reported any negative value as 0", "None of the three, as exceedance reverses every label it touches"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The median is the 50th percentile under either reading, so P50 is the same both ways. P90 is the low case, the 10th percentile of the paths, which a plain-percentile reader would take as the high case, and P10 is its mirror. nonNegative changes values and leaves labels alone. Exceedance swaps the two outer labels and leaves the middle one where it was.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Through the platform''s quantile, the sorted values 1 to 10 have a 90th percentile of what, the figure a P10 (high) would take?', '["9, the 9th smallest, because a whole index reads the value at that position alone", "10, the largest of the ten, the only value at or above the 90th percent mark", "Whatever linear interpolation gives 90 percent of the way along the sorted values", "9.500000: with idx 9 whole and ten values, an even count, the 9th and 10th smallest are averaged"]'::jsonb, 3, 'idx = 10 x 0.9 = 9, a whole number with n even, so the rule averages the 9th and 10th smallest: 9.500000, the course''s table figure. Reading the idx-th alone is no case of this rule. The largest value is not the rule''s answer. Linear interpolation is the numpy default, a different rule the platform does not use.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Which seed values will the bootstrap run on without a refusal?', '["Whole numbers from 0 to 4294967295, both ends included", "Whole numbers from 1 upward, since seed 0 would leave the stream empty", "Any number at all, rounded down to a whole seed before the stream starts", "Only seeds the course has used, 11 and 12, so that runs can be checked"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The boundary table: seed 0 and 4294967295 accepted, -1 and 2.5 refused, in the words "seed must be a whole number from 0 to 4294967295". Seed 0 returns an interval. A fractional seed is refused outright. Any seed in the range is legal; the course''s seeds are simply the ones it prints figures for.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Raising the teaching run from 1000 to 10000 paths moves the P10 (high) at step 12 from 324.803671 to 314.738791. Has the interval moved toward the point forecast of 169.556510 in any way that matters?', '["Yes: with enough paths the P10 (high) closes on the point forecast, so 100000 paths would end the gap", "No: more paths steady a percentile, and it stays far above the point forecast at every count", "Yes: the fall shows the 1000 run was biased high, so the 10000 figure is the right one to report", "No, because the high case is capped at twice the point forecast once there are 10000 paths"]'::jsonb, 1, 'More paths steady the percentiles and leave the method and its residuals as they were; the high case at step 12 is 308.081207 at 100 paths, 324.803671 at 1000 and 314.738791 at 10000, far above 169.556510 each time. No count of paths closes the gap, which is set by the replayed residuals. Each figure is right for its seed and nSims. No cap ties a percentile to the point forecast.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'At step 1 of the teaching run the interval is 51.569125 bbl/d wide. What sets that width?', '["phi, fitted at 0.960949, which scales the first step''s residual before it is added", "Path count: 1000 paths always span about 50 bbl/d at the first step of any run", "The spread of the residual pool, since each path holds one draw at step 1", "The distance from the last month''s rate to the point forecast at step 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At step 1 every path is the one-step forecast plus a single draw, so the width reflects the spread of the residuals alone; later steps widen as draws accumulate through the state. phi damps the trend and scales no residual. The path count moves percentiles a little and sets no width. The gap between the last rate and the first forecast is the method''s, and it widens nothing.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A bootstrap call passes `nonNegative` as the word ''yes''. What does the engine reply?', '["Read as true, with negative percentiles clipped as usual", "It ignores the word and falls back to the default, which is true", "It refuses naming `seed`: \"seed must be a whole number from 0 to 4294967295\"", "\"nonNegative must be true or false\", naming the field `nonNegative`"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The switch is a strict true or false, and a word in its place is refused with the field named, in the words quoted. No word is interpreted as true. A value given is checked as given, and the default applies only when the field is left out. The seed has its own rule and its own message.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'Of the five long Ekene wells, which has the steepest Arps decline per month?', '["EKENE-P1, with Di 0.060069 per month", "EKENE-P2, with Di 0.057021 per month", "EKENE-P3, with Di 0.044394 per month", "EKENE-P5, with Di 0.110048 per month"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every Di here is per month, since a month is passed as a day, so they compare directly: EKENE-P5''s 0.110048 is the largest, and the generator stated 0.11 for it. EKENE-P1''s 0.060069, EKENE-P2''s 0.057021 and EKENE-P3''s 0.044394 are all smaller declines per month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'EKENE-P1''s Arps fit returns qi 1202.685523. Which rate is that?', '["The fitted rate at t = 0, which on EKENE-P1 is index 0, the first positive month", "An average of the 48 monthly rates, which the Arps fit uses as the anchor of its curve", "Month 47''s rate, from which the Arps forecast then runs forward one step at a time", "The first month''s rate as recorded, 1176.100000, carried into the fit unchanged"]'::jsonb, 0, 'qi is the rate at t = 0, and t = 0 is the first positive value; on EKENE-P1 that is index 0. It is a fitted value, so it differs from the recorded 1176.100000 of month 0, and the generator stated 1200. The fit anchors on no average, and the forecast comes from the fitted curve itself.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'EKENE-P2, EKENE-P4 and EKENE-P5 return hyperbolic b values of 0.950000, 0.400000 and 0.100000. Why is each a multiple of 0.05?', '["The engine rounds every fitted b to the nearest 0.05 before it is returned to the caller", "The hyperbolic fit tries b on a grid from 0.05 by 0.05 to 2, so b is always a grid value", "The generator stated those exact b values, and the fit recovers each one without error", "A b off the 0.05 grid would make Di negative, and the engine refuses such fits"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "hyperbolic by a b grid from 0.05 by 0.05 to 2 on q^-b", so every hyperbolic b is a grid point, as printed; as returned it can differ from the printed decimal in its last bits, as EKENE-P1''s does. Nothing is rounded. The generator stated 0.3, 0.7 and 0.1 for these wells, and only EKENE-P5''s matches. The refusal for no fit concerns Di <= 0 on the line, unrelated to the grid.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'At step 12 the Arps forecast of EKENE-P1 is 156.514309 and the damped point forecast 169.556510. How should a reader take the two figures?', '["As a percentile of simulated Arps paths set beside a point forecast, the Arps figure being the low case", "As fits of different months, since damped read fewer of them and so lags the decline", "As point forecasts from two different fits, each carried forward from its own fitted curve or state", "As one decline lowered at every step by the zero months that the Arps fit dropped"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both are point forecasts: the Arps figure comes from the fitted decline curve, qi, Di and b, and the damped figure from the fitted final level and trend, each step''s change phi times the one before. Neither is a percentile. Both methods read all 48 months. EKENE-P1 has no zero months, and the Arps fit dropped none. Which of the two forecasts is better is a question for a backtest on the same origins, where on EKENE-P1 from first origin 30 arps ranks first.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'A colleague pastes EKENE-P1''s Di of 0.060069 into a tool that reads decline per year. What goes wrong?', '["Nothing, since a decline rate carries no unit and means the same in every tool that reads it, per month or per year", "The tool reads a monthly figure as a yearly one, wrong by the change of unit; the engine''s Di is per month", "Nothing, because the engine converts Di to a yearly figure before it prints the fit''s results", "The tool reads a daily figure as a yearly one, since fitArpsModel measures its time in days"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Month k is passed as day k, so Di is per step and a step is a month; carried into a tool that reads per year, the same number means a far gentler decline. A decline rate always carries a time unit. The engine converts nothing. fitArpsModel counts in days, but the days it is handed are months, so the figure is per month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'After EKENE-P2''s workover, holt ranks above arps by MASE from first origin 28. What does the course give as the reason the Arps baseline lags there?', '["Its least-squares fit on each window includes the months before the uplift, so it forecasts the lifted well high", "The Arps fit is held from the first window when refit is true, so it misses the uplift altogether", "The shut-in zeros are fitted as rates in the Arps windows, dragging every fitted curve toward zero", "Arps cannot fit any window after a shut-in, so its row is left unranked and holt wins by default"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Arps fit is a regression on every positive month of each training window, the months before the uplift included, and its mean error after the workover is -23.147528 bbl/d: it forecasts high. Arps is refitted on every window whatever refit says. The zeros are dropped before the fit. The arps row has numbers here, MASE 0.605968, and ranks third.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'A comparison is passed `arpsModel` ''Linear''. What does the engine reply?', '["The arps row fitted as a straight line through each window''s rates", "A comparison with the arps row unranked and its error naming the model it could not fit", "\"modelType must be ''Auto-Select'', ''Exponential'', ''Harmonic'' or ''Hyperbolic''\", naming `modelType`", "\"arpsModel must be ''Auto-Select'', ''Exponential'', ''Harmonic'' or ''Hyperbolic''\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'A model the engine does not offer is refused with the field `arpsModel` named, in the words quoted; the same four names are the ones `arpsForecast` takes as `modelType`, and that field and its message belong to `arpsForecast` alone. No straight-line model exists in the decline curve engine''s list. An unranked arps row comes from a failed fit on a window; an unknown name stops the call. Nothing is substituted.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Why does every ranking the engine returns put the lowest metric first?', '["The listed order runs from the simplest method to the most complex one in the list", "Lower metrics come from the methods that have fewer parameters to fit on each window", "Every metric it ranks by is an error, and a smaller error is better", "Sorting lowest first keeps arps at the end of every ranking"]'::jsonb, 2, 'mae, rmse, mape, smape and mase are all errors, so lowest first puts the best method first. The listed order only settles ties. A metric''s size says nothing about the number of parameters. arps is last only inside a tie band; on EKENE-P1 it ranks first.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'On EKENE-P2 months 0 to 21, before the shut-in, from origins 10, 13, 16 and 19 with horizon 3, which method ranks first by MASE?', '["arps, at 0.389192", "holt, at 0.483573", "damped, at 0.504549", "ses, at 1.006754"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Before the shut-in the ranking by MASE is arps, holt, damped, ses, with arps at 0.389192; on these origins the baseline wins. holt''s 0.483573 and damped''s 0.504549 come next, and ses''s 1.006754, above 1, is last.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'EKENE-P2 compared from first origin 28 has a MAPE on every row, while from first origin 12 every MAPE is null. What makes the difference?', '["From origin 28 the engine drops the shut-in months from the actuals before it divides", "From origin 28 no scored actual is a shut-in month, since months 22 to 24 fall inside the training windows", "MAPE is null only when a training window holds a zero, and from 28 none of them does", "From origin 28 the engine replaces each zero with the month before it, which it cannot do from 12"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MAPE divides by each actual and is null when any actual is 0. From origin 28 the scored months run from 28 on, after the shut-in, so every actual is positive; from origin 12 the shut-in months 22 to 24 are among some origins'' actuals. Nothing is dropped or replaced. Zeros in a training window do not touch MAPE, which is taken on the actuals.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'The stated series 900, 880, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 870, 860, 850, 845 is compared from first origin 6, horizon 2, step 2, and ranked by mae instead of mase. Where does arps end up?', '["Ranked last, since its mae is taken as the largest possible", "Ranked by mae, since only its MASE was null in the first run", "First, since the other methods cannot fit through six zeros", "Still unranked, since its row carries no metrics at all"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The arps row with no fit carries an error and no metrics, so it is unranked whatever the metric; the smoothing methods rank ses, damped, holt either way. An unranked method is never placed last. The missing Arps fit is at origin 6, so every metric of that row is absent. The smoothing methods take a zero as a value and fit through it.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'A flat series of 50 in every one of 10 months is compared with the methods given in the order holt, damped, ses, ranking by mae. Which order does the engine return?', '["holt first, then damped, then ses, as the call listed them; arps has no place", "damped, ses, holt, the order the golden itself lists", "ses, holt, damped, arps, all four tied at an MAE of 0", "arps, holt, damped, ses, since a flat forecast suits Arps"]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a flat series each smoothing method forecasts every month exactly, so all three MAE figures are 0 and fall inside the tie band; a tie is settled by the order of the call, here holt, damped, ses. The course''s golden lists damped, ses, holt and so returns that order; this call lists another. No Arps curve fits a series with no decline, so the arps row carries no MAE to rank.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'Two methods'' MASE both print 0.571171 in a comparison table. May a reader conclude that they tied?', '["Yes, because the engine''s tie band is set at the printed precision, six decimals", "Yes, and the method listed later in the call then ranks first between the two", "No: figures printing alike at six decimals can still differ by more than 1.00e-12 relative; read the ranking", "No, because two MASE figures can never be equal once each origin has its own scale"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tie band is 1.00e-12 relative, far finer than six decimals, so two figures printing alike may still be ordered by the metric; the ranking the engine returns is the answer, and a printed table cannot settle it. The band has nothing to do with printed precision. Inside a tie the listed order keeps the earlier method first. Equal MASE figures are possible, as the constant-series golden shows for MAE.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'A caller passes the methods ses and arps to `compareWithArps`. What happens?', '["Accepted, with arps compared twice, once as listed and once more as the baseline the engine adds at the end", "Refused, \"methods[1] must be ''ses'', ''holt'' or ''damped''\", since arps is added by the engine itself", "Accepted, with the listed arps moved to the end of the list and treated as the one baseline", "Refused, \"methods must be a non-empty array of ''ses'', ''holt'' and ''damped''\", as arps spoils the list"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The list takes only ''ses'', ''holt'' and ''damped''; anything else is refused by its position, counted from 0, so arps in second place is `methods[1]`. The engine always adds arps itself, last, so it never needs listing. Nothing listed is moved or doubled. The empty-list message is for a list with nothing in it, and this one had two entries. The repeat message, "methods[2] repeats ses", is for a smoothing method listed twice.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'A comparison is run with `refit` and `m` left out. What settings does it run with?', '["refit false and m 12, the settings a yearly seasonal comparison of the wells would use", "Refitting on, with MASE scaled by the month a year before, m 12, since that is the default", "Held parameters and m 1, the Arps fit held from the first window too", "refit true and m 1, and the Arps baseline is refitted on every window either way"]'::jsonb, 3, '`compareWithArps` refits by default, as `backtest` does: its signature reads `refit = true` and `m = 1`. The lag 12 is a seasonal naive that a caller may choose; a declining well has no season. With refit false only the smoothing methods hold their first window''s parameters; Arps is refitted at every origin regardless.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'EKENE-P2 compared from first origin 28, horizon 6, step 3 stops at origin 40. Why is there no origin 43?', '["43 + 6 is above 48, so an origin at 43 would leave fewer than 6 actuals", "The origin cap of 5000 allows only five origins at this step", "Origins must be even or a multiple of the first origin, and 43 is neither", "The shut-in months shift the origins, and 43 falls into the gap"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An origin is kept while o + horizon <= n, so every origin has all its actuals; 40 + 6 = 46 fits, and 43 + 6 is above 48. The origin cap is 5000, far above five. Origins run from the first origin by the step, with no parity rule. The shut-in months lie before origin 28 and move nothing.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'On the coarse grid of the fit, in what order are the points scored, and when does a later point replace the best?', '["alpha outermost, then beta, then phi; a later point wins only below best x (1 - 1.00e-12)", "phi outermost, then beta, then alpha; any later point with an equal SSE replaces the best", "In random order from a seeded stream; the lowest SSE wins, and a tie goes to the last point", "alpha, beta and phi together along a diagonal; a later point wins when within 1.00e-12"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The grid is scored alpha outermost, then beta, then phi, and a later point must be below best x (1 - 1.00e-12) to replace the best, so a tie keeps the earlier point. Nothing in the fit is random; seeds belong to the bootstrap alone. The grid is a full product of 11, 11 and 5 values, and being within the band is a tie, which keeps the earlier point.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'How many points does the coarse grid score for a damped fit with every parameter free?', '["121, from 11 alpha values and 11 beta values", "1067, every SSE evaluation the damped fit makes", "605, from 11 alpha values, 11 beta values and 5 phi values", "11, the alpha values alone, as beta and phi are left to the compass search"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The grid has 11 points for ses, 121 for holt and 605 for damped, derived from 11 alpha values, 11 beta values and 5 phi values. 121 is holt''s grid, without phi. 1067 is the whole damped fit on EKENE-P1, grid and compass search together. The grid covers every free parameter, and the compass search starts from its best point.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, '`atBounds` on EKENE-P2''s damped fit reads alpha = 1, beta = 0. What does that list mean?', '["Both parameters were given by the caller and held fixed at exactly the values that the list reports", "Both fitted parameters ended exactly on an edge of their box, and each is a fitted value", "The fit did not converge, and the list names the parameters that stopped the search", "Both were clipped from values outside the box, and the fit that follows is refused"]'::jsonb, 1, '`atBounds` lists every fitted parameter that ended exactly on a bound of its box, alpha and beta at 0 or 1 here, and a parameter on its bound is a fitted value like any other. Given parameters are listed in `fixed`. Every fit in the course converged, EKENE-P2''s included. A compass trial past an edge is clipped to it, and nothing about a bound refuses a fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'On EKENE-P1, phi 0.5 is given and alpha and beta are left free. Which lists name which parameter?', '["`atBounds` lists phi = 0.5, since it lies below the fitted box of 0.8 to 0.98", "The call is refused, because a given phi must lie inside the fitted box", "`fixed` lists alpha and beta, which are held while phi is searched", "`fixed` lists phi, and `atBounds` lists the fitted beta = 1; the given 0.5 is not in `atBounds`"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A phi below the fitted range can only be given; it is accepted and held fixed, and alpha and beta are fitted around it: alpha 0.784159, beta 1.000000, and the fitted beta on its upper bound is listed as beta = 1. `atBounds` names fitted parameters only. A given phi need only be above 0 and at most 1. alpha and beta were left out, so they are fitted.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Why does the compass search''s first step start at 0.05 of each parameter''s range?', '["The smallest step that the grid''s tie band of 1.00e-12 can still tell apart from a step of zero", "Half the grid spacing: the grid steps by 0.1, and the first compass step is half of that", "Matching the lowest b on the Arps grid, so that both searches move by the same steps throughout", "It is 5 percent of the SSE at the best grid point, so the first step scales with the fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stop rule''s description: "The compass step starts at 0.05 of each range (half the grid spacing)", and the grid runs at 0.1. The tie band is a relative test on SSE, unrelated to step size. The Arps b grid is another engine''s search. The step is a fraction of each parameter''s range, and the SSE plays no part in its size.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'What is the fewest set of values `accuracy` will score?', '["Two actuals, so that MASE always has a naive difference to divide the errors by", "Twelve actuals, one full season, so that the lag of 12 can apply to them", "One actual and one forecast, since a single error can be scored", "As many actuals as the training series has months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The length rules give 1 actual and 1 forecast; with no actuals the call is refused: "actual has 0 values: at least 1 actual is needed". MASE''s scale comes from the insample series, and when it cannot be formed MASE is null with its reason while the other metrics are numbers. The lag m is 1 by default and applies to the training series. The actuals need not match the training length.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Is a ses backtest on EKENE-P1 from first origin 2, horizon 6, step 6 accepted?', '["Yes, since a ses backtest takes 2 training values as its first origin", "No, because every backtest needs 3 training values, whatever the method", "No, because origin 2 leaves fewer than 6 actuals on a series of 48 months", "Yes, but only after the engine moves the first origin up to 3 on its own"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A backtest''s first origin is the method''s fitting length, 2 for ses and 3 for holt and damped, so origin 2 is accepted for ses; the refusal for origin 45 gives the ses range as "from 2 to 42". 3 training values is the comparison''s rule and holt''s. Origin 2 leaves 46 months after it. The engine never moves an origin; it accepts or refuses.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'What is the common alternative to passing month k as day k to fitArpsModel, and why did the engine not take it?', '["Passing each month as 30 days; the engine avoids it because Di would then come out per year", "Calendar days; fitArpsModel reads days and a step is a month, so month k as day k keeps one axis of steps", "Passing month k as year k; the engine avoids it because fitArpsModel refuses yearly time", "Weekly steps; the engine avoids them because four weeks do not make a calendar month"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conventions table names calendar days as the alternative, and the stated reason for the choice is that fitArpsModel reads days and a step is a month; passing month k as day k keeps the Arps fit and the smoothing methods on one axis of steps. Passing months as 30 days would give a Di per day, a different unit. Neither yearly nor weekly steps are named by the course.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'What reason does the engine give for reading its percentiles with lib/stats quantile rather than linear interpolation?', '["Linear interpolation fails on an even count of paths, and 1000 is even", "lib/stats quantile gives higher percentiles, which suit a high case", "Linear interpolation needs a seed of its own, which would break reproducibility", "It is the platform''s one quantile, so every percentile on the platform is read the same way"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conventions table: "the platform''s one quantile", against linear interpolation, the numpy default. Interpolation works on any count; it can simply give a different value on the same sorted values. Neither rule is chosen for its direction. No quantile rule draws random numbers; the seed belongs to the paths.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Which sMAPE does the engine compute?', '["Without absolute values in the denominator, on 0 to 100, following the numpy default for percentages", "Absolute values in the denominator, on 0 to 100, with a month where actual and forecast are 0 dropped", "Absolute values in the denominator, on 0 to 200, a 0/0 term scoring 0, after Hyndman and Koehler 2006", "The same as MAPE, divided by 2, so that a shut-in month is scored as 100 and never left out"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: absolute values in the denominator, 0 to 200, and a 0/0 term scoring 0, citing Hyndman and Koehler 2006; the alternatives are without absolute values, or on 0 to 100. A 0/0 term scores 0; nothing is dropped. sMAPE divides by the actual and the forecast together, which is why a shut-in term scores 200.000000 while MAPE is null.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'A note on EKENE-P4 gives damped''s backtest MASE and nothing else from the comparison. What does the course''s note rule say is missing?', '["The Arps baseline''s MASE on the same origins and the ranking with the metric it is by", "Nothing, since MASE with its lag is the one figure a note has to carry", "The damped method''s in-sample MSE over the months it was fitted on, which ranks methods better than MASE", "The R2 of the damped fit, which shows how much of the decline it explains"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The note names the backtest''s MASE with its m beside the Arps baseline''s on the same origins, and the ranking with its metric; it never reports a smoothing method''s metrics without the Arps row. An in-sample MSE scores months the fit already saw, and the note judges on held-out months. R2 belongs to the Arps fit and scores no forecast.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'What source of uncertainty do the teaching run''s intervals leave out?', '["The spread of the residuals, which the engine replaces with a normal distribution", "Uncertainty in alpha, beta and phi, which are fitted once and held on every path", "Draw-to-draw noise, which the seed removes by making the paths identical", "Earlier draws'' effect on later steps, since each step restarts the state"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine builds no parameter uncertainty into the bootstrap: the parameters are fitted once and held, and the spread of the paths is the spread of the replayed residuals and nothing else. No distribution is assumed; the residuals are replayed as fitted. The seed makes a run reproducible while its paths still differ from one another. The simulated value updates the state, so earlier draws carry forward.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'EKENE-P6 has 3 months. Which holt calls can the engine run on it?', '["All three, as 3 months are enough for any holt call", "The bootstrap and the fit, while the backtest is refused", "The fit alone, with 1 scored error; the bootstrap and a holt backtest are refused", "None, since a series of 3 months is refused outright"]'::jsonb, 2, '`fitSmoothing` fits holt on the 3 months with 1 scored error. `forecastIntervals` refuses because 1 scored residual is below the 2 it resamples, and `backtest` refuses because holt needs 3 training values plus the horizon. So only the fit runs, and a note says which tests could not be run and why.'),
  ('forecastml', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'A figure is quoted as "the forecast for EKENE-P1". What does the course''s vocabulary ask it to add?', '["Nothing, since a forecast means any estimate of the well''s future rate", "Its P50, since a forecast in this course is the middle of the paths", "Its error against the Arps baseline, which defines a forecast here", "The method it comes from, since a forecast is the point forecast of one fitted method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The vocabulary rule: a forecast is the point forecast of one fitted method, named with its method; a percentile is named as a percentile. Any estimate of the future is what the word means elsewhere, and the course narrows it. The P50 is a percentile of the bootstrap paths and differs from the point forecast. An error is actual minus forecast, a separate word.');
